<h2 id="canonical">Canonical date expressions</h2>
<p>
    The date parser reduces every input to a small canonical language before producing a TimeSpan: EDTF where EDTF can say it
    (<code>1957/1959</code>, <code>1850~</code>), and a few keywords where it cannot (<code>late 195X</code>, <code>century 19</code>,
    <code>born 1606</code>, <code>fl 1480/1510</code>, <code>period:edo</code>, <code>4.5 Ga ago</code>). The same canonical form
    always realises to the same TimeSpan under a given profile, which is what makes the corpus testable. The language is documented in
    <a href="https://github.com/tomcrane/linked-art-net/blob/de-pmc/docs/canonical-expression.md">docs/canonical-expression.md</a>.
</p>

<h2 id="edtf">EDTF</h2>
<p>
    The <a href="https://www.loc.gov/standards/datetime/">Extended Date/Time Format</a> is the Library of Congress profile of
    ISO 8601-2019 for dates as they occur in cultural heritage records: intervals (<code>1606/1669</code>), approximate and
    uncertain dates (<code>1850~</code>, <code>1850?</code>), unspecified digits (<code>195X</code>), seasons (<code>1969-21</code>
    is spring 1969) and open ends (<code>../1936</code>). Many catalogues and the Linked Art community already exchange dates in it.
    The playground shows the EDTF form of a reading whenever one exists; period names, floruit dates, lifecycle pairs and
    the vaguer bands have no EDTF equivalent, so that row is absent for them and the canonical expression carries the meaning.
</p>

<h2>Profiles</h2>
<p>
    Conventions differ: "19th century" is 1800 to 1899 in a museum label, and 1801
    to 1900 to anyone counting strictly from year 1, since there was no year zero. "Early" is a third to
    some and a half to others; US archives write month first. A <strong>profile</strong> fixes these choices. The repository ships
    <code>default</code>, <code>library</code>, <code>archive-us</code>, <code>catalogue-quarters</code>, <code>medieval-manuscripts</code>
    and <code>multilingual</code>, and a profile advisor that proposes settings from a sample of your strings.
</p>

<h2 id="corpus">The corpus and its sources</h2>
<p>Every entry in the corpus states where it came from. Imported collections, each under a licence compatible with this project's MIT licence:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong><a href="https://github.com/jeresig/node-yearrange">yearrange</a></strong> (MIT), the
        <a href="https://github.com/jeresig/node-yearrange/blob/master/test/date-tests.json">test data</a> of John Resig's year-range
        parser: museum catalogue dates, largely from Japanese print collections.
        <a href="/dates/corpus?file=imported/yearrange.json">In the corpus browser</a>.</li>
    <li><strong><a href="https://github.com/alexduryee/timetwister">timetwister</a></strong> (MIT), the
        <a href="https://github.com/alexduryee/timetwister/blob/master/spec/dates_spec.rb">specification examples</a> of Alex Duryee's
        date normalisation gem, written at the New York Public Library: archival date forms.
        <a href="/dates/corpus?file=imported/timetwister.json">In the corpus browser</a>.</li>
    <li><strong><a href="https://www.getty.edu/research/tools/vocabularies/ulan/">Getty ULAN</a></strong>
        (<a href="https://opendatacommons.org/licenses/by/1-0/">ODC-By</a>), biography descriptions from the Union List of Artist Names,
        fetched from the <a href="https://vocab.getty.edu/sparql">Getty SPARQL endpoint</a>.
        <a href="/dates/corpus?file=imported/getty.json">In the corpus browser</a>.</li>
    <li><strong><a href="https://www.loc.gov/standards/datetime/">EDTF specification</a></strong> (public domain), the Library of
        Congress's examples, level by level. <a href="/dates/corpus?file=imported/edtf-spec.json">In the corpus browser</a>.</li>
</ul>
<p>
    Two further collections were seeded from this project's own history and carry the repository's licence: the date strings
    found in the Paul Mellon Centre pipeline's outputs, and the test strings of Digirati's earlier <code>py_datetime</code> prototype
    for University of the Arts, London (UAL).<br/>
    Named period tables (Japanese eras, Chinese dynasties, British periods, archaeological ages) and the seven language lexicons
    state their provenance inside each file. Full notices are in
    <a href="https://github.com/tomcrane/linked-art-net/blob/de-pmc/corpus/imported/THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md">THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md</a>,
    and the corpus itself, with its <a href="https://github.com/tomcrane/linked-art-net/blob/de-pmc/corpus/README.md">README</a>
    and the <a href="https://github.com/tomcrane/linked-art-net/blob/de-pmc/docs/08-corpus-contributions.md">guide to contributing</a>,
    is in the repository under <a href="https://github.com/tomcrane/linked-art-net/tree/de-pmc/corpus">corpus/</a>.
</p>

